    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-human-b06-friction-blister","source_ref":"source:static-human-b06-nhs-blisters","anchor":{"section":"How to prevent blisters"},"note":"NHS says blisters are mostly caused by friction and that they protect damaged skin. No concrete wound, severity, infection, recovery time, or outcome.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-human-b06-friction-protection","source_ref":"source:static-human-b06-nhs-blisters","anchor":{"section":"How you can treat a blister yourself"},"note":"NHS advises covering a blister and not using the shoes or equipment that caused it until it heals. This supports limited protective context, not a treatment procedure or outcome.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-human-b06-head-warning","source_ref":"source:static-human-b06-nhs-head-injury","anchor":{"section":"When and where to get medical help"},"note":"NHS lists inability to stay awake and seizure after head injury as reasons for immediate help. No diagnosis, cause, prognosis, or individual outcome.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-human-b06-head-repeated-vomiting","source_ref":"source:static-human-b06-uhsussex-head-injury","anchor":{"section":"When to seek out urgent medical help"},"note":"UHSussex lists vomiting more than once and a seizure among reasons to seek urgent help after head injury. No single observation identifies an injury or cause.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-human-b06-visual-rest","source_ref":"source:static-human-b06-nhs-dry-eyes","anchor":{"section":"Causes of dry eyes; How to treat dry eyes yourself"},"note":"NHS notes long unbroken screen viewing as a dry-eye risk and advises breaks to rest eyes. It supports qualitative visual-comfort context, not a historical task rule, threshold, diagnosis, or performance result.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-human-b06-dim-light","source_ref":"source:static-human-b06-nei-healthy-vision","anchor":{"section":"Eye Care: Myths and Facts"},"note":"NEI states dim-light reading can make eyes feel tired but is not harmful. No assurance about disease, injury, visibility, or a concrete person's vision.","review_status":"approved"}
